# Generated by scripts/emu/walk-script.py from
#   /private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-yona-dev-photomancer-lp2025/a82f90f8-28f6-4834-b789-e63a3613469e/scratchpad/p4/cap4/walk.uart.bin
# Every request waits for the answer to the one before it, and every
# request is written 64 bytes at a time 30 ms apart, so the
# whole walk is a function of guest time and no write outruns the
# 128-byte RX FIFO. Do not hand-edit: re-run the capture and
# regenerate.
# Firmware: fw-esp32v3 esp32,server,float-f32 (the defaults) + frame-dump, built at 8b76ce5ab; direct load, blank flash (so the device runs its memory FS).
# Project: a SCRATCH copy of projects/test/shader-oracle whose output endpoint is rewritten ws281x:local:D10 -> ws281x:local:IO18. The DOM-Z-102 has no D10; scripts/m4-hardware-walk.sh's prepare_project makes the same copy for a board, and the committed project is never forked.
# Link: UART0 — the classic has no USB-Serial-JTAG. Captured through scripts/emu/uart-tcp-proxy.py at 64 B per 5 ms of WALL clock against `lp-emu-esp32v3 --uart0 tcp:`.
# 30 ms of GUEST time between chunks on replay, and the reason is measured rather than inherited: the io_task drains 64 B per 1 ms pacer turn into a 128-byte RX FIFO, so anything from ~5 ms up is safe for the FIFO — but the pacing decides WHEN, in guest time, the window-spill defect in the crate README ("A frame three ways") kills the guest. Measured on this image: gap 20 ms = 1 frame and no lit one, gap 25 ms = 0 frames, gap 15 ms = 13 lit, gap 30 ms = 21 lit frames past the first. 30 ms is the value that gets the walk to its evidence; it is a run parameter, not a tolerance, and when the defect is fixed any of them will do. A much SLOWER host is not safe at all: at 400 ms a chunk a partial line is flushed stale and the guest dies in the parser.
# Request 11 (projectRead) waits for the loadProject ANSWER, where the C6's walk waits for '[shader-node] compilation succeeded'. The classic acknowledges the load and THEN compiles, exactly as the C6 does, but the compile is one 16 ms slice of guest time here and the 30 ms chunk gap covers it; either needle works.

after "[RECOVERY] boot complete (first frame served)" "M!{\"id\":18446744073709551615,\"msg\":\"hello\"}\n"
